  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 1257 AM CDT Fri Oct 16 2026\n\nA very strong cold front is moving int the northwestern part of\nthe CWA this morning. Along the front a few showers will remain\npossible through early morning but the main impact will be\nstarkly lower temperatures and gusty winds. Wind gusts 30-35 \nmph are occurring immediately behind the front before tapering \nback to 20 to 25 mph. Today will feel much different from \nyesterday with highs likely struggling to break 60 during the \nday underneath fairly persistent low clouds. \n\nBehind the large shortwave trough pushing this front strong\nupper ridging builds in. Fairly breezy northwest winds are\nexpected to persist through much of the day Saturday before the\nsurface reflection of the upper ridge axis passes overhead\nSaturday night. At that point strong radiational cooling\nconditions set up with dewpoints in the low to mid 30s. Frost\nlooks likely for a sizable chunk of the CWA, with a freeze very\nmuch possible in southern Illinois and southwest Indiana. For \nmessaging clarity will not issue a Frost Advisory with this \nshift but one will likely be required for at least part, if not \nall, of the CWA by Saturday night. \n\nDeep layer ridging looks to control the pattern for the\nremainder of the forecast period. With dry, and settled\nconditions with low humidity and large diurnal temperature\nswings, highs in the mid 70s lows in the mid 40s which is close\nto seasonal norms.",
